@@ -0,0 +1,55 @@
+---
+id: dl-002-applypatch-error-semantics-immutability-and-root-handling
+type: decision-log
+title: "applyPatch error semantics, immutability and root handling"
+status: approved
+---
+
+## Context
+
+Implementing `applyPatch(document, patch)` (RFC 6902) raised choices the RFC leaves to the
+implementer: how strictly to validate a patch before applying it, how to guarantee the input
+document is never mutated (including for the root path `""`, which has no parent member/element to
+splice), what "fails" means for `test`, and whether `copy` may let the new location alias the source.
+
+## Decision
+
+1. **Structural validation up front.** Before any operation runs, `applyPatch` checks `patch` is an
+   array and that every operation is an object with one of the six `op` values and the fields that
+   op requires (`path: string` always; `value` present for `add`/`replace`/`test`; `from: string` for
+   `move`/`copy`). A malformed operation throws immediately, same as `resolvePointer` throwing on a
+   malformed pointer — this is a boundary the function must not trust.
+
+2. **Per-operation failure semantics**, matching RFC 6902 literally rather than being lenient:
+   - `add`: the parent location must already exist; for an array, the index must be `0..length`
+     (insert) or `-` (append) — anything else (out-of-bounds, non-canonical) throws.
+   - `remove` / `replace`: the target location itself must already exist; throws otherwise.
+   - `move` / `copy`: `from` must resolve to a value (reusing `resolvePointer`'s lookup). `move`
+     additionally rejects `from` being a proper prefix of `path`, per RFC 6902 §4.4 ("a location
+     cannot be moved into one of its children"); `copy` has no such restriction since the source is
+     left untouched.
+   - `test`: compares structurally (object key order irrelevant, array order relevant, as JSON
+     equality is usually defined); any mismatch — or `path` not resolving at all — throws, aborting
+     the patch, since there is no "the test just didn't match but we continue" mode in this API.
+
+3. **Immutability via upfront clone + root handling.** `applyPatch` clones `document` once with
+   `structuredClone`, then applies every operation to that clone in place and returns it; the
+   original `document` is never touched, even if a later operation throws partway through the
+   patch (the half-mutated clone is simply discarded). To avoid special-casing the root pointer
+   (`""`, which names the whole document and so has no real parent to splice/delete on), the clone
+   is kept inside a one-key holder object (`{ doc: <clone> }`); every operation's parent/key pair is
+   then computed uniformly (`["doc", ...tokens]`), including when `tokens` is empty. `copy` deep
+   clones the value it reads before inserting it at the new location, so mutating one copy later
+   cannot affect the other through a shared reference; `move` does not need this since removing the
+   source leaves only one live reference to relocate.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- `applyPatch` is safe to call with a document the caller still holds a reference to elsewhere;
+  nothing it does is visible outside the returned value.
+- Operations are applied strictly in array order with no rollback of earlier operations in the same
+  call if a later one throws — callers that need all-or-nothing application across retries must
+  snapshot the document themselves before calling `applyPatch` (which they can already do safely,
+  since `applyPatch` never mutates it).
+- Because validation happens before any mutation, a malformed operation anywhere in the array (even
+  the last one) prevents every operation in the patch from applying, not just the invalid one.
