@@ -0,0 +1,38 @@
+---
+id: task-002-implement-json-patch-rfc-6902-applypatch
+type: task
+title: "Implement JSON Patch (RFC 6902) applyPatch"
+status: approved
+---
+
+## Request
+
+Implement JSON Patch (RFC 6902) with its six operations: `add`, `remove`, `replace`, `move`, `copy`
+and `test`. Export `applyPatch(document, patch)` from `src/index.ts`. It applies the patch (an array
+of operations) to a JSON document and returns the result as a new value, without mutating its input.
+It throws when the patch is invalid, or when one of its operations fails.
+
+## Plan
+
+- Add `src/index.ts` exporting `applyPatch(document, patch)` plus the `Operation`/`JsonPatch` types:
+  - Validate the patch is an array and each operation is an object with a recognized `op` and the
+    fields RFC 6902 requires for that op (`path` always; `value` for `add`/`replace`/`test`; `from`
+    for `move`/`copy`); throw on anything malformed before applying any operation.
+  - Clone the input document up front (`structuredClone`) and apply every operation to the clone in
+    order, in place, returning the clone; the caller's `document` is never touched.
+  - Reuse `resolvePointer`'s pointer-parsing rules for `path`/`from` syntax and for read-only lookups
+    (`test`, and the source of `copy`/`move`).
+  - `add`: parent must exist; array index must be `0..length` (insert) or `-` (append); `remove`/
+    `replace`: the target itself must already exist. `move`/`copy`: `from` must resolve to a value;
+    `move` additionally rejects `from` being a proper prefix of `path` (RFC 6902 §4.4). `copy` deep
+    clones the copied value so the new location cannot alias the source. `test` uses structural JSON
+    equality (object key order irrelevant, array order relevant) and throws on mismatch.
+- Add `test/apply-patch.test.ts` (`node:test`): a happy-path test per operation plus an error-path
+  test per operation's main failure mode, written first and confirmed red before implementing.
+- Document `applyPatch` in `README.md` under "API", alongside `resolvePointer`.
+
+## What was done
+
+Implemented as planned above; see the `dl-002` decision-log entry for the design choices RFC 6902
+leaves open. All new tests pass alongside the existing `resolvePointer` suite; `tsc --noEmit` is
+clean.
